WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:12.000
Romania might be the first country in the
world to legalize corruption.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.450
Yes, you heard that right...

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:16.430
&nbsp;
In January 2017, the Romanian parliament passed

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:23.190
an ordinance which allowed politicians to
accept bribes of almost $50,000 USD.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.720
Without any kind of legal repercussions whatsoever.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.510
&nbsp;
As you can imagine, this infamous ordinance

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:33.770
triggered one of the biggest demonstrations
the country has seen for 20 years.

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:41.409
The 
protests led to the government suspending

00:00:41.409 --> 00:00:42.409
the law temporarily.

00:00:42.409 --> 00:00:45.320
But even after this, protests continued.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.050
&nbsp;
Romanians are rather used to living in Europe’s

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:48.249
most corrupt country.

00:00:48.249 --> 00:00:52.600
And this ignominious decree was the final
straw that broke the camel’s back…

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:53.600
&nbsp;
But the question now is…

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:54.870
How did they get to this point?

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:58.960
What’s going on in a country that could
make its government pass this kind of law

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.739
without feeling any sort of shame?

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:02.780
Well, today we are going to answer this question.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.309
But before we do that, let´s take a look
back at history.

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:11.360
THE EUROPEAN NORTH KOREA
&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:17.189
You can´t understand Romanian history without
knowing about Nicolae Ceausescu: The European

00:01:17.189 --> 00:01:20.729
Kim Jong Un.

00:01:20.729 --> 00:01:27.859
Now, other neighbouring countries like Hungary
or Bulgaria have suffered communist dictatorships

00:01:27.859 --> 00:01:28.859
too.

00:01:28.859 --> 00:01:31.509
But the story of Romania is quite special.

00:01:31.509 --> 00:01:34.110
&nbsp;
Since Ceausescu came to power in 1974, his

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:38.430
country has been isolated from the rest - even
from the Soviet Union itself.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:43.409
Soon, even food started to become scarce.

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:47.430
While citizens had their meals rationed by
the state, Ceausescu spent money building

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:48.700
pharaoh-style palaces.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:54.590
For instance, here is the Parliament of Bucharest,
built in 1983 and named the biggest administrative

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:56.840
building in the entire world.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:02.210
This is especially amazing if we take to account
that the Romanian population is no bigger

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:04.740
than 22 million inhabitants.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:10.640
During the construction, more than 20,000
people were employed and, around 3,000 died

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.020
of exhaustion.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:13.290
&nbsp;
But not only that!

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:18.250
Ceausescu forced all his citizens to participate
in parades and massive shows to praising his

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:34.090
own image.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:40.190
Just like his North Korean comrades, Ceausescu
was a master in the art of political repression.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:46.350
During the 80s, an estimate of 1 in every
30 Romanians belonged to the political police.

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:49.980
It could be your neighbour, or your hairdresser,
or anyone really...

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:54.960
Meaning anytime you said something bad about
the leader you could end up spending the rest

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.500
of your days in jail, or simply just disappearing.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.810
&nbsp;
Nonetheless, despite all of this, being a

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.900
dictator is not a safe job.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:12.860
This footage is from Ceausescu's last speech,
which took place on December 21st 1989.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:16.560
Look at his facial expression and how it does
starts to tremble.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:23.260
It was at this moment that the audience started
to boo him.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:27.200
&nbsp;
This is how the biggest protest in Romanian

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:28.200
history started.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:32.980
Thousands of citizens stepped out on the streets
waving national flags with the communist symbol

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:34.400
out.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:39.220
Three days later, on Christmas Day of that
year, Nicolae Ceausescu was executed, along

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:42.320
with his wife, by his own party colleagues.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:50.900
His last words were “History will revenge
me”.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.090
&nbsp;
And now you might wonder…

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:54.110
well, what happened next?

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:58.740
The truth is, in such an oppressive regime
as Ceausescu's, there were no opposition leaders

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:00.100
whatsoever.

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:03.760
Just to make a comparison, in Poland, when
the communist regime fell, there were powerful

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:09.770
leaders like Les Walesa, who had much popular
support and helped write a brand new constitution

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:10.770
for the country.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:13.750
Nonetheless, who do you think succeeded Ceausescu
in Romania?

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:17.690
Well, that would be his own party comrades.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:27.340
More exactly, the man you see on the screen
now, his name is ION ILIESCU

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:31.730
As you can imagine, this government was not
willing to give up the luxury they had during

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:33.330
Ceausescu's times.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:39.060
So, as always happens in cases like this,
the Romanian regime changed… but just a

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:40.060
little!

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:43.630
Of course, the new constitution allowed other
parties to run for elections, and the old

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:48.410
communist party changed its name to the Social
Democratic Party, but the high ranking officials

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:54.140
that ruled the country during the dictatorship
kept their power during the 1990s and the

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:55.140
2000s.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:57.691
&nbsp;
So, as you know, if power corrupts, absolute

00:04:57.691 --> 00:04:59.940
power corrupts absolutely.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:09.840
For a better understanding: In 1995, 50% of
the Polish economy was already in private

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:10.840
hands.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.419
But in Romania, this figure was only 35%.

00:05:13.419 --> 00:05:16.900
&nbsp;
So, let’s have a look at an example…

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:19.000
Take two car manufacturers.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:24.910
Czechoslovakia had a state owned factory,
ŠKODA that was privatized in 1992 by sharing

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:26.990
the ownership among the workers.

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:32.330
It’s Romanian counterpart, DACIA, was not
privatized until 1997.

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:35.680
And instead of doing it by sharing it, it
was simply sold to RENAULT GROUP.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:37.830
&nbsp;
In other words, during the 90s and the 2000s,

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:43.020
the ruling elite in Romania did whatever they
could in order to keep control of the economy

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:44.139
and the country.

00:05:44.139 --> 00:05:54.660
It was only though these actions that they
were able to keep a hold on their power.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:59.060
&nbsp;
But wait just a second!

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:02.130
Because corruption was present at lower levels
as well...

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:07.280
In a country that had been so used to scarcity,
it was impossible to get anything without

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:08.280
a little bribe.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:10.190
Want to go to the doctor?

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:12.520
Bring a box of cigarettes with you.

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:14.290
What to have electricity at home?

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:17.120
A whisky bottle and everything is done!

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:22.639
And that is how a country corruption a way
of life.

00:06:22.639 --> 00:06:26.030
&nbsp;
And now you might wonder…

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:29.650
Is it really possible for a country to work
this way?

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:32.010
And the answer is… well...

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:33.010
no.

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:37.020
Romanians saw how other former communist republics
such as the Czech Republic or Poland started

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:42.630
enjoying a Western standard of living while
they kept surviving on less than 400 USD a

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:43.630
month.

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:44.980
&nbsp;
This is why, in the middle of 2000, Romania

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:50.050
requested to become a member of European Union
so they could get access to some of those

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:52.070
sweet sweet EU funds.

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:57.960
And Brussels said “OK, dude, I’ll I let
you in, but with one condition...” and that

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:00.720
condition was…

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:04.699
THE NATIONAL ANTICORRUPTION DIRECTORATE
&nbsp;

00:07:04.699 --> 00:07:09.770
In the year 2002, the Romanian Government
founded an agency to chase corruption - the

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:12.530
“National Anti Corruption Directorate”.

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:17.600
For it’s first years, this agency was directed
by prosecutors connected to party leaders.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:20.990
So this was the Romanian way of saying “Hey
Brussels!

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:23.590
Look how great were doing!

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:26.020
We’re cleaning up all this corruption!”

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:33.150
But then again, nothing changed…

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:36.990
&nbsp;
That was until the year 2013.

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:40.930
That year is when a women came to lead the
directorate.

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:49.540
A woman ready to face everything and everyone,
and her name is LAURA CODRUTA KOVESI.

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:58.430
Romanians seem to have been impressed by the
activities of DNA: according to one recent

00:07:58.430 --> 00:08:04.669
poll, 60% of people said they trusted the
agency, while only 11% said they trusted parliament.

00:08:04.669 --> 00:08:07.270
&nbsp;
In just 2 years, Kovesi has jailed more than

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:10.919
1100 politicians accused of corruption.

00:08:10.919 --> 00:08:15.870
During this time years, Romanians have discovered
all the dirty things their ruling class were

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:16.870
hiding.

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:18.889
And there are no little things!

00:08:18.889 --> 00:08:23.990
According to an estimate, corruption accounts
for 15% of Romanian GDP.

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:28.280
Virtually, all the public sales had some kind
of bribe involved.

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:33.880
Even the Microsoft licenses that the civil
servants use had a cost of over $3 million

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:38.810
which were used to pay bribes to different
Government officials.

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:43.589
&nbsp;
Then there was the case of a judge who was

00:08:43.589 --> 00:08:49.540
paid around $700,000 in order to declare someone
innocent who was actually guilty.

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:55.449
And look, the list, it just goes on on on.

00:08:55.449 --> 00:08:58.589
&nbsp;
But since the year 2013, all of these criminals

00:08:58.589 --> 00:09:03.019
have been filling up Romanian jails… to
the point that some of the prisons have no

00:09:03.019 --> 00:09:04.290
space anymore.

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:06.029
But not only that...

00:09:06.029 --> 00:09:10.660
Romanian society itself has started to realize
how damaging corruption is.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:13.389
&nbsp;
But the real turning point came a little bit

00:09:13.389 --> 00:09:16.310
later with this news…

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:20.259
“Romanian nightclub fire leaves 27 dead”
&nbsp;

00:09:20.259 --> 00:09:24.470
This news triggered a wave of protest all
across Romania.

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:29.259
Apparently, this fire could have been avoided
if the club would have met safety regulations.

00:09:29.259 --> 00:09:34.410
But since the owner had bribed the local authorities,
he could open the club while breaking the

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:35.410
rules.

00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:40.589
This is how a platform came to be, called
CORUPTIA UČIDE, or “CORRUPTION KILLS”.

00:09:40.589 --> 00:09:46.339
Through social media, Romanians started to
organize themselves in protest groups against

00:09:46.339 --> 00:09:47.339
corruption.

00:09:47.339 --> 00:09:49.779
&nbsp;
Oh, but wait just a moment, because the best

00:09:49.779 --> 00:09:53.579
is yet to come...

00:09:53.579 --> 00:09:56.869
THE BIGGEST DEMONSTRATIONS IN ROMANIAN HISTORY
&nbsp;

00:09:56.869 --> 00:10:02.970
On January 18th, 2017, at 10PM, the Social
Democratic Government and its Prime Minister,

00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:06.399
Sorin GRINDEANU, passed an emergency bill.

00:10:06.399 --> 00:10:10.149
And yes, they did it at night, when everyone
was about to go to bed.

00:10:10.149 --> 00:10:12.509
So, what did that decree say?

00:10:12.509 --> 00:10:14.720
&nbsp;
Well, it said that politicians who accepted

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:21.209
bribes for less than 44,000 Euros did not
have to face jail time or pay any fines...

00:10:21.209 --> 00:10:22.309
nothing.

00:10:22.309 --> 00:10:25.899
It was absolutely fine with the law to be
bribed up to that amount.

00:10:25.899 --> 00:10:34.629
And they discussed this law while debating
the national budget as normal, so no one would

00:10:34.629 --> 00:10:36.350
notice the change.

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:38.569
&nbsp;
So now you might be wondering, who can possible

00:10:38.569 --> 00:10:40.800
dare to pass such a bold law?

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:41.800
I mean…

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:46.470
what could possibly be the official reason
behind the decriminalization of bribery?

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:51.970
Well… this was the reason given by the Romanian
Prime Minister, SORIN GRINDEANU.

00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:57.179
&nbsp;“he wants to pass the ordinances into
law to ease overcrowding in prisons.”

00:10:57.179 --> 00:11:02.220
Yes… this was the actual reason given by
the current Romanian Government.

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:04.040
Boom, just like that.

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:07.429
According to estimates, this ordinance would
free over 3000 corrupt politicians.

00:11:07.429 --> 00:11:10.980
Yes, while it is true that Romanian jails
are overcrowded, in a country where money

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:16.179
is drained from the system by bribery, it
should not be too difficult to find funds

00:11:16.179 --> 00:11:17.379
for new prisons.

00:11:17.379 --> 00:11:21.490
Also, the fact that the majority of the jailed
politicians belonged to the government party

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:25.779
makes it even more suspicious…

00:11:25.779 --> 00:11:27.850
&nbsp;
Well half a million Romanians thought so too,

00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:33.809
and took to the streets of Bucharest minutes
after the decree was passed.

00:11:33.809 --> 00:11:39.930
Through the previously established, “Corruption
Kills” platform, citizens learned about

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:41.649
this news instantly.

00:11:41.649 --> 00:11:44.350
And boy didn’t they like it...

00:11:44.350 --> 00:12:04.009
And that is how the biggest protest in Romanian
history started.

00:12:04.009 --> 00:12:05.820
The protest kept going for days.

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:09.039
Not only in Bucharest, but in most Romanian
cities…

00:12:09.039 --> 00:12:11.290
&nbsp;
The first victim of this unrest was the Justice

00:12:11.290 --> 00:12:14.980
Minister, who was forced to resign on February
9th 2017.

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:19.309
As we mentioned the protests led to the government
retiring this new law, although they have

00:12:19.309 --> 00:12:21.240
already admitted that this is just temporary.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:26.679
Further, the brand new Justice Minister, TUDOREL
TOADER has a clear mission: to justify the

00:12:26.679 --> 00:12:33.259
firing of the general prosecutor, LAURA CODRUTA
KOVESI, the woman who jailed over 1000 politicians.

00:12:33.259 --> 00:12:36.540
The government claim that her agency is running
a witch hunt.

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:43.259
But, they still haven’t found a legal way
to fire her… yet.

00:12:43.259 --> 00:12:49.730
“After evaluating all the constitutionality,
legality, and opportunity factors, I determined

00:12:49.730 --> 00:12:55.470
that, at the moment, it’s not advisable
to start the institutional mechanism to revoke

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:58.439
the general prosecutor and the DNA chief prosecutor,”&nbsp;
&nbsp;

00:12:58.439 --> 00:13:02.879
At the same time, Grindeanu´s government
is confronted by two different sides.

00:13:02.879 --> 00:13:05.399
On the one hand, civil society is surveying
his movements.

00:13:05.399 --> 00:13:09.980
On the other hand, the European Union is demanding
that he stop obstructing the anti-corruption

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:10.980
prosecutor.

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:15.300
And of course, all of the political elite,
inheritors of Ceausescu's regime, are turning

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:18.720
to classic conspiracy theories to protect
themselves…

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:22.740
According to them, George SOROS and the ROTHSCHILD
family would are behind the protesters.

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:28.050
And they say, of course, that corruption is
not actually a big deal in Romania at all.

00:13:28.050 --> 00:13:29.629
&nbsp;
Well, with or without conspiracies, we are

00:13:29.629 --> 00:13:40.269
still talking about an existential crisis
in Europe’s third poorest country.

00:13:40.269 --> 00:13:42.389
&nbsp;
So now the question is: Do you think Romania

00:13:42.389 --> 00:13:44.709
should jail all their corrupted politicians?

00:13:44.709 --> 00:13:48.839
Or do you think that, instead, Grindeanu is
actually doing something right by decriminalizing

00:13:48.839 --> 00:13:49.839
bribery?

00:13:49.839 --> 00:13:57.829
You can leave your answer on the comment section
below!

